# 0x02 — 20 GOOGLE DORKS (fully explained)

Google dorking uses advanced search operators to surface content that's indexed but not meant to be easily found. Below, each dork includes **what it does**, **why it works**, and **a worked example**.

### 1. Scope enumeration
```
site:target.com
```
Lists every page Google has indexed under the domain. This is your baseline map of what's publicly visible. *Example:* `site:tesla.com` returns thousands of pages — press releases, investor docs, careers pages — giving you a feel for the site's structure before you even visit it.

### 2. Non-standard subdomain discovery
```
site:target.com -www
```
Excludes the main `www` subdomain, surfacing things like `dev.target.com`, `staging.target.com`, or `internal.target.com` that Google happened to crawl.

### 3. Sensitive document harvesting
```
site:target.com ext:pdf | ext:docx | ext:xlsx
```
Finds Office/PDF files — often internal reports, org charts, or policy docs that get uploaded to a public-facing CMS by accident. *Example:* `site:target.com ext:xlsx "employee"` has historically surfaced HR spreadsheets left in unlinked but indexed directories.

### 4. Open directory listings
```
site:target.com intitle:"index of"
```
`intitle:"index of"` is the signature of an Apache/Nginx auto-generated directory listing — meaning someone left directory browsing enabled. You can literally browse the raw file tree.

### 5. Exposed admin/login panels
```
site:target.com inurl:admin | inurl:login
```
Surfaces login portals that are indexed (ideally they shouldn't be — a well-configured site would `robots.txt`-disallow or noindex these).

### 6. Leaked environment/config files
```
site:target.com filetype:env "DB_PASSWORD"
```
`.env` files sometimes get deployed into public web roots by mistake. If Google indexed one, it may contain database credentials, API keys, or secret tokens in plaintext.

### 7. Internal document leakage
```
site:target.com intext:"internal use only"
```
Searches the *body text* of indexed pages/documents for phrases that signal the content wasn't meant for public eyes.

### 8. Paste-site credential dumps
```
site:pastebin.com "target.com"
```
Pastebin (and similar sites like Ghostbin) are common dumping grounds for leaked credential lists, source code snippets, or breach data. Searching for the target's domain inside these sites surfaces third-party leaks.

### 9. Hardcoded secrets in public repos
```
site:github.com "target.com" password
```
Developers accidentally commit API keys, database strings, or hardcoded passwords referencing the target domain. This dork surfaces those commits/repos.

### 10. WordPress footprinting
```
site:target.com inurl:wp-content | inurl:wp-admin
```
Confirms WordPress usage and can reveal plugin names in `wp-content/plugins/` paths — letting you cross-reference against known-vulnerable plugin versions later.

### 11. Default/misconfigured server pages
```
intitle:"welcome to nginx" site:target.com
```
A default Nginx/Apache welcome page being indexed means a server was likely spun up and never properly configured — a soft signal of shadow IT or forgotten infrastructure.

### 12. Exposed log files
```
site:target.com ext:log
```
Log files can leak internal IPs, usernames, stack traces (which reveal software versions), or even session tokens.

### 13. Exposed API endpoints
```
site:target.com inurl:"/api/" filetype:json
```
Finds indexed raw JSON responses from API endpoints — sometimes revealing internal object structures, user IDs, or endpoints not documented anywhere public.

### 14. Employee email harvesting
```
"target.com" intext:"@target.com" filetype:xls
```
Spreadsheets containing the company's email domain in plaintext — often contact lists or distribution lists — are gold for building a phishing target list or guessing the email naming convention (`first.last@`, `flast@`, etc.).

### 15. Employee enumeration via LinkedIn
```
site:linkedin.com/in "target company"
```
Surfaces individual employee profiles indexed by Google (works even without a LinkedIn account/login), useful for org-chart building and social engineering pretext research.

### 16. Backup file exposure
```
site:target.com inurl:backup | inurl:.bak
```
Backup files (`.bak`, `.old`, `backup.zip`) left in web-accessible directories often contain full source code or database dumps.

### 17. Historical cached content
```
cache:target.com
```
Pulls Google's last cached snapshot of a page — useful if the live page has since been taken down or modified (though Google has been deprecating this operator, the Wayback Machine is now the more reliable fallback, see Tool #—Wayback).

### 18. Exposed cloud storage
```
site:s3.amazonaws.com "target"
```
Searches for S3 bucket names/content referencing the target — misconfigured "public" buckets are one of the most common real-world breach vectors.

### 19. Competitor / infrastructure mapping
```
related:target.com
```
Google's algorithm suggests "related" sites — sometimes surfacing sister companies, subsidiaries, or shared-infrastructure partners you didn't know about.

### 20. Exposed database dumps
```
site:target.com ext:sql | ext:db
```
Raw SQL dump files left in a public directory are a worst-case exposure — full schema and often full data.

**Advanced operators to chain into any of the above:**
| Operator | Use |
|---|---|
| `AROUND(n)` | Finds two terms within `n` words of each other — tighter than a generic AND search |
| `allintext:` | Forces all following terms to appear in the page body |
| `inanchor:` | Searches the anchor text of inbound links — good for finding how a page is referenced elsewhere |
| `-inurl:` | Excludes noisy URL patterns (e.g., `-inurl:blog` to cut marketing content) |
| `numrange:1000-9999` | Searches for a number within a range — useful for finding document/invoice ID patterns |

