# 0x03 — 50 TOOLS (with explained real-world usage)

## 🔍 Passive Recon / OSINT Frameworks (1–10)

**1. theHarvester**
Aggregates emails, subdomains, and employee names from search engines, PGP key servers, and Shodan. *Example:* `theHarvester -d target.com -b all` pulls from every supported source in one pass and outputs a clean list of `@target.com` addresses and subdomains — usually your very first command on a new engagement.

**2. Maltego**
A graph-based link-analysis tool. You drop in a "seed" entity (a domain, email, or person) and run "transforms" (plugins) that automatically pull related entities — DNS records, social profiles, company officers — and draw them as a connected graph. *Example:* seed with a domain, run the "to DNS Name" and "to Email address" transforms, and watch a visual map of the org's digital footprint build itself.

**3. SpiderFoot**
Fully automated OSINT aggregator with 200+ modules. Point it at a domain/IP/email and it runs nearly every passive technique in this guide simultaneously, correlating results into one report. *Example:* `spiderfoot -s target.com -m sfp_dnsresolve,sfp_crt,sfp_shodan` runs a targeted scan combining DNS, cert transparency, and Shodan modules.

**4. Recon-ng**
A modular, Metasploit-styled recon framework with a workspace/module system. *Example:* inside the console, `marketplace install all` then `modules load recon/domains-hosts/hackertarget` pulls hostnames for a domain — everything is stored in a local database for later correlation.

**5. Amass**
OWASP's attack-surface-mapping tool; combines passive sources (certificate transparency, DNS datasets) with active brute-forcing. *Example:* `amass enum -passive -d target.com` gives a pure passive subdomain list with zero direct contact with the target.

**6. Shodan**
A search engine that continuously scans the entire internet and indexes banners from open ports/services. *Example:* search `org:"Target Corp" port:3389` to find every exposed RDP server the company owns — without you scanning a single IP yourself, because Shodan already did.

**7. Censys**
Similar to Shodan but with a stronger focus on certificate and host metadata. *Example:* `services.tls.certificates.leaf_data.subject.organization: "Target Inc"` surfaces every host presenting a cert issued to that org — a great way to find infrastructure that doesn't share the main domain name.

**8. FOCA**
Extracts and analyzes metadata from public documents (PDF, Office files) harvested off a domain. *Example:* point FOCA at `target.com`, let it download every indexed `.docx`/`.pdf`, and it'll extract author names, internal usernames, printer paths, and software versions from the metadata — often revealing the internal domain naming convention (`CORP\jsmith`).

**9. Google Dorks / GHDB (Google Hacking Database)**
The curated, community-maintained database of dork patterns (exploit-db.com/google-hacking-database) mapped to what they typically expose — login panels, error messages, vulnerable software versions.

**10. Wayback Machine (web.archive.org)**
Historical snapshots of any indexed webpage. *Example:* if a page mentioning `api-internal.target.com` was removed today, you can still find it at `web.archive.org/web/2023*/target.com/careers` — old careers pages are a goldmine for leaked tech-stack details in job listings.

## 🧑‍💻 People / Social Recon (11–20)

**11. Sherlock**
Checks a single username's existence across 300+ platforms in one run. *Example:* `python sherlock.py johndoe123` returns every site where that exact handle is registered — instantly pivoting from "one username" to "a dozen linked accounts."

**12. Social-Analyzer**
Similar to Sherlock but adds profile-analysis scoring (confidence that a found profile actually belongs to the target person, using name/photo similarity).

**13. WhatsMyName**
A username-enumeration project/tool (also available as a web app) with a continuously updated site list, useful as a Sherlock alternative or cross-check.

**14. Pipl / Intelius**
Commercial people-search aggregators that compile public records, past addresses, and phone numbers into a single profile — useful for identity verification during an engagement (with appropriate authorization).

**15. Hunter.io**
Given a domain, predicts and verifies the corporate email format. *Example:* `hunter.io/search/target.com` might reveal the pattern is `first.last@target.com` along with a confidence score and a list of already-discovered real addresses — critical for building a phishing-simulation target list.

**16. Have I Been Pwned (HIBP)**
Checks an email or domain against known breach datasets. *Example:* querying `jsmith@target.com` might show it appeared in the "Collection #1" breach, telling you which password (if reused) could be a valid credential-stuffing vector.

**17. LinkedIn + CrossLinked**
CrossLinked scrapes LinkedIn search results (via Google/Bing caching to avoid LinkedIn's own anti-scraping) to bulk-harvest employee names and titles. *Example:* `crosslinked -f "{first}.{last}@target.com" target company` outputs a CSV of guessed real employee emails built from scraped names.

**18. Namechk**
Checks username *and domain* availability simultaneously across many platforms — useful both offensively (impersonation-risk assessment) and defensively (brand protection).

**19. IntelTechniques Tools (Michael Bazzell's suite)**
A curated set of web-based search tools/forms covering everything from phone-number lookups to image metadata — the de facto "OSINT swiss army knife" bookmark page for investigators.

**20. Dehashed**
A searchable, indexed database of breach data (paid access) — lets you search by email, username, password, IP, or even name and get back matched breach records far faster than manually trawling paste sites.

## 🌐 Domain / Infrastructure Recon (21–30)

**21. WHOIS / RDAP**
Domain registration lookup. *Example:* `whois target.com` reveals registrar, creation/expiry dates, and (if not privacy-protected) registrant contact info — sometimes still leaking an internal admin's name or a shared registration pattern across sister domains.

**22. DNSdumpster**
Free web-based DNS recon tool that visually maps a domain's DNS records, mail servers (MX), and hosting providers, and often surfaces subdomains other tools miss.

**23. crt.sh**
Searches Certificate Transparency (CT) logs — since 2018, all publicly trusted SSL certs are logged publicly. *Example:* `crt.sh/?q=%.target.com` returns every subdomain that ever had a certificate issued, including short-lived dev/staging environments long since taken offline but still logged forever.

**24. Sublist3r**
Classic subdomain enumeration tool pulling from multiple search engines and DNS sources in one command: `sublist3r -d target.com`.

**25. Assetfinder**
Fast, lightweight subdomain discovery tool, great for chaining into pipelines: `assetfinder target.com | httpx -silent` to instantly get a list of *live* subdomains.

**26. dnsrecon**
DNS enumeration tool that also tests for zone transfer misconfigurations. *Example:* `dnsrecon -d target.com -t axfr` attempts a zone transfer — if the DNS server is misconfigured, it hands over the ENTIRE zone file (every subdomain and internal record) in one request. This crosses into active territory.

**27. Fierce**
DNS reconnaissance tool built for non-contiguous IP-space discovery — finds nearby/related IP blocks that a standard subdomain scan would miss.

**28. SecurityTrails**
Commercial platform offering historical DNS, WHOIS, and passive-DNS data — lets you see what an IP or subdomain resolved to *last year*, not just today, which is invaluable for tracking infrastructure changes over time.

**29. BuiltWith**
Technology-stack fingerprinting via a browser lookup/API. *Example:* `builtwith.com/target.com` shows the CMS, analytics tools, CDN, and even the specific JavaScript framework version in use — informing which known CVEs might apply.

**30. Wappalyzer**
Browser extension / CLI version of tech fingerprinting, faster for quick manual checks while browsing a target site live.

## 🖥️ Active Recon / Scanning (31–40)

**31. Nmap**
The foundational port/service/OS scanner. *Example:* `nmap -sV -p- target.com` does a full 65535-port scan with service-version detection — this is the classic "what's actually running" step, and it *will* appear in the target's firewall/IDS logs.

**32. Masscan**
Internet-scale port scanner capable of scanning the entire IPv4 space in minutes. *Example:* `masscan -p1-65535 10.0.0.0/8 --rate 10000` — useful for quickly sweeping large authorized IP ranges before doing a deeper Nmap pass on the interesting hosts.

**33. httpx**
Fast HTTP probing tool (from ProjectDiscovery) — takes a list of hosts and tells you which ones are actually alive over HTTP/S, with status codes and titles: `cat subdomains.txt | httpx -title -status-code`.

**34. Nuclei**
Template-based vulnerability scanner — thousands of community-maintained YAML templates check for specific CVEs, misconfigurations, and exposed panels. *Example:* `nuclei -u https://target.com -t cves/` runs every known CVE template against the target in one pass.

**35. Gobuster / ffuf**
Directory and subdomain brute-forcers. *Example:* `ffuf -w wordlist.txt -u https://target.com/FUZZ` hammers the server with requests for every word in a wordlist, revealing hidden paths like `/backup/` or `/api/v2/internal/` that aren't linked anywhere.

**36. Nikto**
Web-server vulnerability scanner that checks for thousands of known dangerous files, outdated server software, and misconfigurations in one automated pass: `nikto -h https://target.com`.

**37. WhatWeb**
Fingerprints web technologies actively by sending requests and analyzing response signatures (headers, cookies, HTML patterns) — more aggressive/thorough than passive fingerprinting tools like BuiltWith.

**38. Aquatone**
Takes a list of hosts, visits each one, and generates a visual screenshot report — critical for triaging hundreds of subdomains quickly by *eye* rather than reading raw HTTP responses one by one.

**39. EyeWitness**
Similar to Aquatone — screenshots web services plus RDP/VNC where possible, and auto-categorizes by likely purpose (login page, default page, etc.).

**40. Netcat ("the network Swiss army knife")**
Manual banner grabbing and raw protocol probing. *Example:* `nc target.com 25` connects directly to the mail server's SMTP port and lets you read the raw banner (often revealing the exact mail server software/version) and manually issue commands like `VRFY` to check if a username exists.

## 🗺️ Geo / Image / Metadata (41–46)

**41. ExifTool**
Extracts embedded metadata from images and documents. *Example:* `exiftool photo.jpg` can reveal GPS coordinates, camera model, and the exact timestamp a photo was taken — a classic geolocation starting point when investigating an image posted on social media.

**42. Google Earth Pro**
Used to cross-reference GPS coordinates or visual landmarks (extracted via ExifTool or spotted in a photo background) against real-world satellite imagery to confirm a location.

**43. Yandex Reverse Image Search**
Widely regarded as the strongest reverse-image search engine for facial matching (better than Google for finding *where else* a specific face/photo appears online) — a staple for verifying claimed identities.

**44. TinEye**
Reverse image search focused on finding the *earliest* known instance of an image online — useful for debunking a photo's claimed recency/context.

**45. Overpass Turbo**
A query interface for OpenStreetMap data — lets you pull structured geographic data (all ATMs in a city, all buildings matching a description) to narrow down a geolocation candidate.

**46. SunCalc**
Calculates sun position/shadow angles for a given date, time, and location — used in advanced geolocation to verify or narrow down *when* a photo was taken based on shadow direction/length visible in the image.

## 🧩 Aggregators / Automation (47–50)

**47. OSINT Framework (osintframework.com)**
Not a tool itself but a categorized, clickable directory of hundreds of OSINT resources organized by data type (username, email, domain, etc.) — the best "I don't know where to start" reference.

**48. Datasploit**
Automates OSINT collection across domains, people, and companies, aggregating output from multiple APIs (Shodan, HIBP, etc.) into one report.

**49. GHunt**
Specifically targets Google accounts — given an email address, it can reveal the associated Google ID, public Maps reviews, YouTube channel, and other linked Google services metadata.

**50. Holehe**
Checks whether an email address is registered on 120+ platforms by abusing each platform's "forgot password" or "sign up" flow response differences — without ever sending an actual email or notification to the target: `holehe target@email.com`.

