# 0x04 — PASSIVE RECON: FULL WALKTHROUGH

**Scenario:** authorized engagement against `example-corp.com`. Goal: map the external attack surface and employee footprint without sending a single packet to their infrastructure.

```
STEP 1 — WHOIS + RDAP
  $ whois example-corp.com
  → registrar, creation date, nameservers (e.g., "ns1.cloudflare.com" tells you they're behind Cloudflare)

STEP 2 — Certificate Transparency sweep
  → crt.sh/?q=%.example-corp.com
  → returns: dev.example-corp.com, api-staging.example-corp.com, vpn.example-corp.com
  → these logged certs may point to environments no longer linked from the main site

STEP 3 — DNS mapping
  → DNSdumpster + SecurityTrails
  → confirms MX records point to Google Workspace (tells you the phishing pretext should mimic Gmail's login UI)

STEP 4 — Dork sweep
  → site:example-corp.com ext:pdf   → finds a 2022 "IT Onboarding Guide.pdf"
  → FOCA metadata extraction on that PDF reveals internal usernames formatted as "e.surname"

STEP 5 — Shodan/Censys pass
  → org:"Example Corp"
  → reveals an exposed Jenkins instance on port 8080 — zero packets sent by you, Shodan already scanned it

STEP 6 — People enumeration
  → CrossLinked scrape of LinkedIn → 340 employee names
  → Hunter.io confirms email format is "e.surname@example-corp.com" with 92% confidence
  → cross-reference against the FOCA-extracted username pattern from Step 4 — they match, confirming the format

STEP 7 — Breach correlation
  → HIBP query on 10 sampled emails → 4 appear in past breaches
  → Dehashed lookup on those 4 → 1 shows a plaintext password pattern ("CompanyName2019!") suggesting a predictable internal password policy

STEP 8 — Historical check
  → Wayback Machine on the careers page → an old job listing for "DevOps Engineer" mentions "our AWS + Jenkins + Terraform stack" — confirms and dates the Jenkins finding from Step 5
```

**Output of this phase:** a full subdomain list, confirmed email format, a named list of real employees, one exposed CI/CD tool, and a plausible password pattern — all without the target's logs showing a single request from you.

