# 0x05 — ACTIVE RECON: FULL WALKTHROUGH

**Scenario:** same engagement, now authorized to move to active scanning against the discovered scope.

```
STEP 1 — Live host confirmation
  $ cat subdomains.txt | httpx -silent -title -status-code
  → confirms which of the passively-discovered subdomains are actually live right now

STEP 2 — Port/service scan on confirmed live hosts
  $ nmap -sV -p- vpn.example-corp.com
  → reveals OpenVPN on 1194 and an outdated OpenSSH banner on 22

STEP 3 — Vulnerability template scan
  $ nuclei -u https://api-staging.example-corp.com -t cves/,exposures/
  → flags a misconfigured CORS policy and an exposed .git directory (exposures/git-config)

STEP 4 — Directory brute-force on flagged host
  $ ffuf -w common.txt -u https://api-staging.example-corp.com/FUZZ
  → surfaces /api/v1/internal/users — an undocumented internal endpoint

STEP 5 — Visual triage across the full live host list
  $ aquatone -ports large -out ./aquatone-report < subdomains.txt
  → screenshot grid lets you eyeball 40 hosts in 5 minutes and flag the 3 that look like internal admin tools accidentally exposed

STEP 6 — Manual verification
  $ nc api-staging.example-corp.com 443 -- (or use openssl s_client)
  → manually confirm the CORS/git findings aren't false positives before they go in the report
```

**Output of this phase:** confirmed live infrastructure, one outdated SSH service, an exposed `.git` directory (potential source-code leak), and an undocumented internal API endpoint — each finding independently reproducible and screenshotted for the report.

